# Never stage everything

Multiple agent sessions work in this repo at the same time, so the working tree routinely contains
edits that belong to someone else. Staging blindly has repeatedly swept unrelated work into commits.

Never run any of these:

```bash
git add -A
git add .
git add --all
git add -u
git commit -a
git commit -am "..."
```

Stage by explicit path instead, listing every file you touched:

```bash
git add src/engine/itemEffects.ts src/ui/GridPicker/ItemPicker.tsx
git commit -m "..."
```

If a file is new, it still gets named explicitly — no wildcards that could match another session's
files.

## Before committing

Run `git status` and confirm the staged set contains only files you edited in this session. If
something you did not touch is already staged, unstage it with `git restore --staged <path>` rather
than committing it.

## If you are unsure who changed a file

Do not guess and do not commit it. Leave it unstaged and tell the user it is dirty, naming the file.
